body:
After playing around, I made a somewhat workable solution, though I feel this is far from a proper fix since the measurements of the keyboard are wrong a lot of the time and constantly checking the keyboard size seems wasteful compared to the OS resizing the window as needed. 

This example also accounts for different DPIs as the virtual_keyboard_get_height returns height unscaled in pixels. 

```gd
# (In the root 2d node of your scene)
var _scale: float = 1

func _ready() -> void:
	if OS.get_name() == "Android":
		var dpi = DisplayServer.screen_get_dpi()
		_scale = (dpi / 160.0)
		get_window().content_scale_factor = _scale

func _process(_delta: float) -> void:
	if OS.get_name() == "Android":
		size.y = (DisplayServer.get_display_safe_area().size.y - DisplayServer.virtual_keyboard_get_height()) / _scale
```

This solution seems like it would work for a game with a HUD but for a GUI app, this has some issues where some UI doesn't restrict itself to the root nodes bounds(right click menus for example).
